WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:05.680
Fans of the Marvel Cinematic Universe love&nbsp;
to dig through their favorite movies for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:10.880
Easter eggs and references, but some hidden&nbsp;
secrets are harder to find than others.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:16.400
Here are a few deep cut Easter eggs that&nbsp;
took audiences multiple years to discover.

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:20.240
As the very first film in the&nbsp;
Marvel Cinematic Universe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:25.120
"Iron Man" has a lot of differences&nbsp;
from the dozens of expansions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:30.320
sequels, and spinoffs that followed it.&nbsp;
There's references to real-world conflicts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:36.960
no team-up action, and — wait a second, that's not&nbsp;
Don Cheadle. One thing the mega-franchise has had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:43.440
since the very beginning, however, is plenty of&nbsp;
Easter eggs and hidden references to reward the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:50.000
most perceptive fans. The hidden details in "Iron&nbsp;
Man" are many, but that doesn't mean they were all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:56.320
easy to find — in fact, one particular Easter&nbsp;
egg took fans a good decade to discover.

00:00:56.320 --> 00:01:01.840
Tony Stark's final adversary in "Iron Man"&nbsp;
is Obadiah Stane, his former mentor and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:06.240
the manager of Stark Industries. He&nbsp;
starts out as a fairly jovial presence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:12.960
propelled by that trademark Jeff Bridges charisma&nbsp;
that even shines through Stane's immaculate suits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:19.760
and chrome dome, but there's a sinister reality&nbsp;
underneath. In fact, Stane was actually the one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:24.160
who planned Tony's kidnapping at the hands&nbsp;
of the mysterious Ten Rings organization&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:30.080
in an attempt to take the CEO position for&nbsp;
himself. In the film's explosive climax, Stane&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.080 --> 00:01:36.000
ends up donning the Iron Monger suit and giving&nbsp;
Tony his first taste of supervillain combat.

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:42.560
In 2018, Reddit user HellotoHorse noticed that&nbsp;
Stane actually foreshadows his true intentions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:48.400
around an hour into the movie, simply by sitting&nbsp;
behind a grand piano and playing a peppy little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:54.800
tune. While the scene might look unassuming, the&nbsp;
man behind the composition Stane is playing is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.800 --> 00:01:59.920
actually Antonio Salieri, an 18th century&nbsp;
composer who was depicted as the killer of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:06.080
Wolfgang Amadeus Mozart in the 1984 Best Picture&nbsp;
winning film "Amadeus." While there's no actual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:11.440
historical evidence that Salieri killed Mozart&nbsp;
in real life, the version of the composer played&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:17.200
by F. Murray Abraham does mirror Stane's&nbsp;
arc in "Iron Man" eerily well. After all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:23.280
both Stane and Salieri are older, less brilliant&nbsp;
talents who envy the prestige of younger,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:27.920
more celebrated geniuses to the point that&nbsp;
they start plotting their respective downfalls.

00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:33.440
"Guardians of the Galaxy Volume 1" is&nbsp;
a treasure trove of hidden references,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:38.880
many of which director James Gunn has long&nbsp;
refused to point out — not even to Chris Pratt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:44.480
and the other stars of his own film. Although&nbsp;
the movie came out in 2014, there may still be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:51.440
secrets just waiting to be discovered, as Gunn has&nbsp;
teased for years that a massive discovery has gone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.440 --> 00:02:56.560
unnoticed for some time. In April 2020,&nbsp;
however, the director did admit that a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.560 --> 00:03:02.240
large part of the final major "Guardians of the&nbsp;
Galaxy" Easter egg had been discovered — some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.240 --> 00:03:06.560
think that it might hint that Peter Quill's&nbsp;
mother Meredith, played by Laura Haddock,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:11.760
is actually a cosmic force known as&nbsp;
Eternity that embodies the universe itself.

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:17.040
The jury is still out on whether or not that's&nbsp;
actually the case, but fans have certainly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:23.040
received their money's worth of Easter eggs from&nbsp;
the blockbuster ensemble comedy. In 2018 — four&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:27.840
whole years after the film's release — Gunn&nbsp;
confirmed a hitherto undiscovered Easter egg,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:34.240
in which Stan Lee's cameo scene subtly features&nbsp;
Lee's famous catchphrase "Excelsior" written in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.240 --> 00:03:39.360
the Skrull language. As recently as February&nbsp;
2021, the director took to Twitter to shoot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:45.040
down a theory that an image of Peter's father,&nbsp;
Ego, was hiding in Meredith's brain scans — and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.040 --> 00:03:49.520
casually revealed that the brain scans&nbsp;
originally contained yet another Easter egg,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.520 --> 00:03:55.600
which may or may not actually be in the final&nbsp;
cut. And that's not even counting "Guardians of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.600 --> 00:04:01.680
the Galaxy Volume 2," which has its own host&nbsp;
of secrets for inquisitive fans to uncover.

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:04.560
"After all these years, I've found you."

00:04:05.360 --> 00:04:10.480
February 2nd, 2018 was a good day&nbsp;
for "Doctor Strange" director Scott&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:15.760
Derrickson — an eagle-eyed fan had finally&nbsp;
managed to spot one of his favorite Easter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.760 --> 00:04:20.640
eggs that he had hidden in the film. As he&nbsp;
retweeted the discovery, Derrickson commented,

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:24.480
"I've waited two years for&nbsp;
somebody to notice this."

00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:29.840
Twitter user @TimDLydon was the first to point&nbsp;
out the Easter egg, which reveals that one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.840 --> 00:04:34.800
the most dramatic events of the movie — the&nbsp;
car accident in which Doctor Strange severely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:41.040
injures his hands — happens on a very particular&nbsp;
date. As Doctor Strange decides which of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:45.760
expensive timepieces to wear to the conference&nbsp;
he would end up crashing en route to, a quick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.760 --> 00:04:52.000
glance at his watch face reveals the date to be&nbsp;
February 2nd: the holiday known as Groundhog Day.

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:56.960
While Groundhog Day is a very real holiday&nbsp;
in which a rodent in Pennsylvania predicts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.960 --> 00:05:03.040
the weather for the upcoming season, it's also&nbsp;
the name of the legendary 1993 time loop comedy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.040 --> 00:05:08.000
starring the equally legendary Bill Murray.&nbsp;
As a weather man sent to Punxsutawney to cover&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:14.160
the annual festivities, Murray's character Phil&nbsp;
Connors finds himself reliving Groundhog Day over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.160 --> 00:05:21.920
and over again, doomed to spend eternity reliving&nbsp;
February 2nd unless he can somehow find a way out.

00:05:21.920 --> 00:05:24.800
The reference is more than just an allusion to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.800 --> 00:05:29.200
comedy classic — it's also a subtle&nbsp;
hint at the good doctor's future.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.200 --> 00:05:35.040
After all, Doctor Strange eventually wins his&nbsp;
seemingly hopeless face-off against the mighty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.040 --> 00:05:40.640
Dormammu by trapping the powerful villain in&nbsp;
a Groundhog Day-style time loop, dooming it to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.640 --> 00:05:46.400
live their confrontation over and over again&nbsp;
until it becomes frustrated and gives up.

